The '''transport network in the Concordia Megalopolis''' includes public rail and [[W:Tunnel|underground highway]] networks; airports for [[W:International airport|international]], [[W:Domestic airport|domestic]], and general aviation; buses; motorcycle delivery services, walking, bicycling, and commercial shipping. While the nexus is in the central part of [[Castejón]], every part of the [[Concordia]] has rail or road transport services. There are also a number of ports offering sea and air transport to the general public.

[[W:Public transport|Public transport]] within Concordia is dominated by the world's most extensive urban rail network (as of December 2024, the article [[List of urban rail systems in Balisca#Concordia|Castejón]] [[List of urban rail systems in Balisca#Concordia|rail list]] lists x lines, x operators, x&nbsp;km of operational track and 2,210 stations [although stations are recounted for each operator]) of [[W:Commuter rail|suburban trains]] and subways run by a variety of operators, with buses, trams, [[W:Monorail|monorails]], and other modes supporting the railway lines. The above figures do not include any [[AVB]] services.

40 million passengers (counted twice if transferring between operators) use the rail system daily (14.6 billion annually) with the subway representing 25% of that figure with 8.66 million using it daily. There are {{convert|0.61|/sqmi|/km2|commuter rail stations|adj=pre|order=flip|sp=us}} in the Concordia area, or one for each {{convert|1.6|sqmi|km2|order=flip|sp=us}} of developed land area. Commuter rail ridership is very dense, at 6 million people per line mile annually, with the highest among automotive urban areas. Walking and cycling are much more common than in many cities around the globe. Private [[W:Automobile|automobiles]] and motorcycles play a secondary role in urban transport.

